-- title:
--   Eq. (A.16) — every bounded solution equals $u_N$ for all $N\ge1$
-- statement:
--   For every bounded solution $W$ of (1.1)–(1.2) and all $N\ge1$, $W(t,x)$ equals
--
--   $$
--   u_N(t,x)=E_t^x\Big[\sum_{i=1}^Ne^{-r(\theta_i-t)}Y_{\theta_i-}h(v_{\theta_i-},X_{\theta_i-})+\int_t^{\theta_N}e^{-r(s-t)}Y_{s-}f(v_{s-},X_{s-})\,ds+e^{-r(\theta_N-t)}Y_{\theta_N}W(v_{\theta_N},X_{\theta_N})\Big].
--   $$
--
--   Letting $N\to\infty$ gives the representation (1.6) and hence uniqueness.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: bounded smooth domain → open, connected, bounded, with a global $C^\infty$ defining function $\rho$; reason: equivalent for bounded domains; the paper gives no degree of smoothness, read as $C^\infty$
--   - encoding: $f$ uniformly Hölder on $\bar Q$ with exponent $\alpha$ → Hölder for the metric of $\mathbb R\times\mathbb R^d$ with an existential $\alpha\in(0,1]$; reason: with $\alpha$ existential this and the parabolic Hölder class coincide on the bounded set $\bar Q$
--   - reading: $d$-dimensional balls, open or closed not specified → open balls; reason: the two readings agree, since $\varepsilon$ is arbitrary and balls can be slightly enlarged
--   - reading: measurability of $(t,x)\mapsto\nu_{t,x}$ with respect to the weak convergence topology → Borel for the topology of `FiniteMeasure` on $Q$; reason: this is the direct meaning of the phrase
--   - encoding: the process $(v,X)$ with $\theta_{i+1}=\inf\{s>\theta_i: X_{s-}\in\partial\mathcal O\text{ or }v_{s-}=T\}$ on a fixed filtered space → any `Realization`: the filtered space is part of the data, $\theta_{i+1}$ is the end of a cycle in $Q$ with left limit in $\partial_pQ$, stochastic integrals are `HasItoIntegral`, the conditional law is given $\mathcal F_{\theta_i-}$ with $\mathcal F_{\tau-}$ generated by the sets $A\cap\{s<\tau\}$; reason: same object: first hit written without an infimum of a possibly empty set; Mathlib has no $\mathcal F_{\tau-}$
--   - encoding: $f(v_{s-},X_{s-})$ and products over $\theta_j$ in $Y$ → $f(v_s,X_s)$ under $ds$ and `finprod`; reason: the left limit differs from the value at jump times only, a Lebesgue null set; only finitely many $\theta_j$ lie below $s$ almost surely
--   - generalization: $|h(s,x)|\le K_h\cdot s$, (2.16) → $|h(s,x)|\le K_h\cdot T$ on $\partial_pQ$; reason: (2.16) implies this bound because $s\le T$, and the proofs use only this bound (p.23)
--   - encoding: the expectation in the statement → integrability of the functional is asserted explicitly; reason: the paper's expectation presupposes it; without it a Bochner integral defaults to $0$
--   - reading: the claim of Step 2 names no range for $(t,x)$ → all $(t,x)\in\bar Q$; reason: the range of (A.11) and (A.15), from which the induction starts
-- source:
--   Dai, Kou, Yang, A Stochastic Representation for Nonlocal Parabolic PDEs with Applications, SSRN 3541591, eq. (A.16), p.27

import Definitions.Def_NonlocalFK_Realization

open MeasureTheory Set Filter

universe u

namespace NonlocalFK

/-- Step 2 of the proof of Theorem 3.1, (A.16) (Dai, Kou, Yang, SSRN 3541591, p.27): for every bounded
solution `W` of (1.1)–(1.2) and all `N ≥ 1`, `W(t, x)` equals
`u_N(t, x) = E_t^x[∑_{i=1}^N e^{−r(θᵢ−t)} Y_{θᵢ−} h(v_{θᵢ−}, X_{θᵢ−})
  + ∫_t^{θ_N} e^{−r(s−t)} Y_{s−} f(v_{s−}, X_{s−}) ds + e^{−r(θ_N−t)} Y_{θ_N} W(v_{θ_N}, X_{θ_N})]`.

**Formalization Note.** The claim is stated on `Q̄`, the range of (A.11) and (A.15) on which the
induction starts, for every realization started at `(t, t, x)`. -/
theorem eq_A_16 {d : ℕ} (m : Model d) (hS : m.Standing) (h22 : m.Assumption22) {Kh : ℝ}
    (hG : m.Growth Kh) (W : ℝ × E d → ℝ) (hW : m.IsSolution W) :
    ∀ N : ℕ, 1 ≤ N → ∀ p ∈ m.Qbar, ∀ (Ω : Type u) [MeasurableSpace Ω]
      (R : Realization m p.1 p.1 p.2 Ω),
      Integrable (R.truncatedPayoff W N) R.P ∧ W p = ∫ ω, R.truncatedPayoff W N ω ∂(R.P) := by sorry

end NonlocalFK
